Now we’re getting into the difference between trusting someone, trusting your understanding of someone, and trusting the performance of trustworthiness.

Those are three very different things.

Consider your three people:

* Someone you know you can’t trust. You may understand their behavior exceptionally well. You don’t trust their intentions, but you might trust your ability to predict them.
* Someone you think you can trust. You have confidence, but perhaps you’re trusting your interpretation more than their demonstrated behavior.
* Someone who’s proven trustworthy. You have evidence across previous interactions. But even that evidence depends on the circumstances in which they were tested.

Here’s the paradox:

You can be more certain about an untrustworthy person than an apparently trustworthy one, without being safer relying on them.

And the person who’s proven trustworthy? You still have to ask what they’ve proven, under which conditions, and at what cost to themselves.

Someone who’s honest when honesty is convenient hasn’t necessarily demonstrated what they’ll do when honesty becomes expensive.

Now, The Prestige.

In The Prestige, the magician and the audience enter a peculiar agreement.

The magician promises an extraordinary experience.

The audience knows they’re being deceived.

And yet they participate willingly because the deception serves a mutually understood purpose.

The audience trusts the magician to deceive them in the right way.

That’s different from trusting someone to tell the truth.

And prestige has its own double meaning here. Social prestige can make someone appear trustworthy before they’ve demonstrated anything. The prestige of a magic trick is its extraordinary final reveal.

One is a reputation. The other is an outcome.

Neither necessarily tells you what happened behind the curtain.

There’s another layer: trust versus predictability.

You can trust a rival to pursue their interests.

Trust a friend to protect yours.

Trust a professional to honor an agreement.

And trust a magician to fool you.

All four can be rational, provided you understand the relationship.

But here’s where it gets interesting:

A person can be trustworthy in one register and untrustworthy in another.

A loyal friend might be terrible at keeping secrets. A dishonest competitor might honor a contract because their incentives depend on it. A prestigious leader might behave impeccably in public and differently in private.

So perhaps the real mastery is not deciding whether someone is trustworthy as an absolute identity.

It’s knowing what you can safely trust them with, how much uncertainty remains, and whether your model changes when the stakes change.

And your “both maybe” is important.

You can simultaneously trust somebody’s demonstrated competence, distrust their motivations, appreciate their company, and maintain boundaries.

Those aren’t necessarily contradictions.

That brings us back to our discussion about seeing in multiple registers.

The trick isn’t always knowing who’s lying. Sometimes it’s recognizing which agreement everyone believes they’re participating in.

Because in The Prestige, the audience wants the illusion.

But the magician’s real sacrifices aren’t necessarily part of what the audience understands.

And sometimes the most revealing thing about trust is not what someone shows you.

It’s what they’re willing to sacrifice to preserve what they’ve shown you.

Prestige can earn attention. Only conduct under meaningful conditions can earn justified trust.